// CONTENTS
//   vir::ktx2   Generic reader/writer for KTX 2.0 files holding uncompressed,
//               lossless texel data. Knows nothing about GPUs or vir textures
//   vir::       encodeKtx2, decodeKtx2, isKtx2: the bridge between KTX2 file
//               data and vir's TextureBuffer2D/3D and CubeMapBuffer
//   Everything else lives in vktx2.cpp and is invisible to includers.
//
// SUPPORTED
//   formats     R, RG, RGBA x {UNORM8, UINT8, UINT32, SFLOAT32, UINT64, SFLOAT64}
//   shapes      1D, 2D, 3D, mip levels, array layers, cube maps (6 faces)
//   compression Zstandard (KTX2 "supercompression" scheme 2, lossless) or none
//   Files written here pass Khronos' validator (`ktx validate`) and load in
//   libktx; files libktx writes in these formats are read here. GPU
//   block-compressed formats (BC*, ASTC, ETC*) and Basis Universal are not
//   supported.
//
// COMPRESSION
//   Files are written zstd-compressed by default (WriteOptions can turn it
//   off); compressed and uncompressed files are both read. vktx2.cpp
//   includes "thirdparty/zstd/zstd.h": add zstd's single-file zstd.c (a C
//   file) to the build, with zstd.h and zstd_errors.h next to it. Files
//   compressed with other KTX2 schemes (zlib, BasisLZ) are rejected with an
//   error.
//
// TEXEL DATA LAYOUT
//   Each entry of ktx2::Texture::levels holds one mip level, tightly packed
//   (no row padding), ordered layer -> face -> z slice -> row -> texel,
//   little-endian. For a single (non-array, non-cube) texture this is exactly
//   what glGetTexImage returns and glTexImage*D expects with pack/unpack
//   alignment 1 (alignment 4 is equivalent for texel sizes of 4+ bytes).
//
// ROW ORDER
//   KTX's default orientation is "first row = top of the image", while
//   OpenGL treats the first row as the bottom. The "KTXorientation" metadata
//   records which one a file uses ("ru" = rows go up, i.e. OpenGL convention,
//   "rd" = rows go down). Cube maps are the exception: OpenGL's cube map
//   faces start from the top row, as KTX's do. encodeKtx2/decodeKtx2 handle
//   all of this for you.

